Of course Wilson probably wouldn't even have bothered finishing a
Moorcock novel.

The trouble with Wilson as a critic of *any* sf is that he was opposed
to the entire field. Realism, incuding the whacked out forms in
Modernism, but *not* incuding science fiction or fantasy, was his
thing. The trouble with Moorcock, I guess, is that he has too limited
a range of taste. He can appreciate pulp writers like Burroghs,
Brackett or Howard, but Tolkien is doing something quite different and
is actually more literary and less naive than Moorcock's literary
heros. That his criticism misfires so totally shows he did not
understand what Tolkien was doing at all. The idea that Moorcock was
giving the intellectual's response to Tolkien is an absurdity.


